DISABLED: this mapping destroys the early bootstrap page tables.

Why it was wrong
----------------
The V=P (TTBR0) bootstrap table maps the physical memory that the boot
arguments describe, starting at the kernel's physical base, with virtual ==
physical.  On lavender the boot arguments describe 768 MiB from the window
base (phone) or 512 MiB (QEMU), and the panel sits outside that range
(phone 0x9D450900, QEMU 0x7C7A0000).

The mapping below called create_bootstrap_mapping with the frame buffer's
physical address as the *virtual* address, which lands in L1 index 0 -- the
same L1 slot the RAM identity mapping already uses.  It therefore replaced
the RAM's L1 entry with a fresh L2 table that held a single 2 MiB block (the
count argument of that macro counts 2 MiB L2 entries, not 1 GiB blocks), so
the whole RAM identity mapping disappeared and the kernel died on its first
instruction fetch after the MMU was enabled:

    Taking exception 3 [Prefetch Abort]
    ...with FAR 0x4b1eb40c   (kernel entry + 0x1f84)
    ...to EL1 PC 0xfffffff0073e6000

How to map the panel correctly
------------------------------
Do not touch L1 index 0 of the V=P table.  Map the panel in the KVA (TTBR1)
table instead, at an alias virtual address that uses a free L1 slot (the
kernel image occupies slot 511), e.g.

    alias = 0xffffff0000000000 + (aligned_pa & 0x7fffffffff)

with the 2 MiB-aligned base of the panel mapped as a block (and a second
block when the panel crosses a 2 MiB boundary).  Code that runs with the MMU
on then reaches the panel through the alias, while the early ladder (which
runs with the MMU off) keeps using the physical address from the boot
arguments.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/start.s
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/start.s
@@ -623,6 +623,27 @@
  	// the early debugger rather than corrupting memory or forging a map.
  	b Lbootstrap_tables_exhausted
 Lbootstrap_tables_ready:
+	/*
+	 * Map the video frame buffer.
+	 *
+	 * The kernel reaches the panel long before the video console exists, so
+	 * the frame buffer is not mapped by the kernel's own page tables yet.  The
+	 * boot arguments carry the physical address the firmware gave the panel;
+	 * map it identity in the low (V=P) table so that early code, a probe or a
+	 * panic can reach the screen.  One 1 GiB block covers any panel.
+	 */
+	mov	x13, x1				// the V=P L1 table
+	mov	x14, x2				// the free table pointer
+	ldr	x0, [x20, #0x28]		// boot args Video[0]: the frame buffer
+	cbz	x0, 1f
+	mov	x1, x0				// identity: virtual = physical
+	mov	x2, #1				// one 1 GiB block entry
+	mov	x3, x13				// the V=P L1 table
+	mov	x4, x14				// the advanced free table pointer
+	create_bootstrap_mapping x0, x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6, x7, x8, x9
+	mov	x2, x4				// the advanced free table pointer
+1:
+
